1. Introduction
The study of roughness in turbulent flows was pioneered by Hagen [1] and Darcy [2] who found that roughness affected pressure drop by increasing the drag force and blockage effects [3]. The formation of the boundary layer over surfaces significantly influences
transport phenomena such as heat and momentum transfer. The roughness of these surfaces can significantly affect the boundary layer flow and thus influence heat and momentum transfer over the fluid-surface interface. Fig. 1 illustrates the boundary layer profile over smooth and rough surfaces. At the leading edge of the plate, flow is laminar; until at some point flow instability occurs initiating a laminar-turbulent transition. For a turbulent boundary layer over a smooth surface, a thin layer of laminar flow known as the viscous sublayer forms along the entire length of the surface, as shown in Fig. 1 (a). For the same flow over a rough surface Fig. 1 (b), the roughness increases flow instability close to the wall which can lead to increased localised turbulence disrupting the viscous sublayer, thereby generating the roughness sublayer and affecting pressure drop and heat transfer.
