Surface roughness may be a result of the manufacturing processes [4] and the normal use of a product (wear and tear). It may also result from mechanisms including erosion, corrosion and deposition processes such as in gas turbines [5,6], ice accretion on aircraft wings [7 9], deposit in combustion chambers [10], and biofouling on ship hulls and marine machinery [11,12]. These types of roughness can compromise the safety, performance, and efficiency of systems, mainly due to the change in the turbulent structure as well as momentum and energy transfer. For example, in a review study on gas turbines, Bons [5], reported that natural degradation and deposition processes affected the surface roughness enough to reduce the performance of both compressor and turbine. “Atmospheric Boundary Layer” (ABL) flows is another area, where flow over terrain, plant and urban canopies (Fig. 2 (a)) act as roughness which influences meteorological and local climate predictions [16 20]. Relevant reviews in ABL include Raupach et al. [21] on theoretical and experimental studies of turbulent flow boundary layers, Raupach & Tom [22] and Finnigan [17] on flow studies over plant canopies, and Roth [23] and Barlow and Coceal [24] on ABL over cities. Although they are occasionally compared with the high Reynolds
number limit of industrial rough-wall turbulent flows, the study of geophysical flow is beyond the scope of this review paper. Intentionally manufactured roughness features are employed for drag reduction and aerodynamic performance improvement [25 30] as well as heat transfer enhancement [30 33]. Dimples on golf balls [34,35], sharkskin denticles on aircraft surfaces [25,36] provide drag reduction, while heat transfer enhancement in roughened solar air heaters was reported by Sharma and Kalamkar [37], Gawande et al. [38], and Alam and Kim [39]. Roughness can improve the wettability of the surface hydrophobicity [40,41] as well as repellency of the hydrophilic surfaces [42]; which in turn, significantly influence the performance of many fluid flow devices such as heat pipes [43,44], desalination system [45 47], etc. Surface roughness can also be incorporated with other heat transfer enhancement methods such as modified fluids, especially those containing nano-particles. These “nanofluids” have been found to further improve the performance of the heat exchangers [48,49] and as a new generation of heat transfer fluids, have shown great promise in heat transfer enhancement [50,51]. It has been proved that geometrical constraints such as surface roughness can influence the thermal performance of nanofluids including via increased area and eddy effects [51 53]. Recent advances in manufacturing, including Additive Manufacturing (AM), permit the fabrication of engineered rough surfaces as a design parameter, allowing more control of the shape and pattern of the roughness elements that may be difficult to produce by traditional methods [54,55]. The application of AM in the fabrication of thermal management systems and aerospace applications has rapidly increased in recent years and is an active research topic. AM processes can leave irregular and random surface roughness, the measurement of which were reviewed by Townsend [56]. Other researchers [57 60] have studied the effects of AM fabricated rough surfaces on fluid flow and heat transfer. As well as experimental studies, Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) has been widely adopted for rough flow studies. Surface roughness can be accounted for in CFD by either including roughness effects in turbulent CFD models, such as wall-modelled LES or Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes (RANS), or direct resolving via using DNS or LES. The adoption of a suitable numerical method depends on the physics of the problem (Geometry, Reynolds number, etc.) and more importantly, the available computational resources. Modern computing capabilities have enabled researchers to perform Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS) of turbulent flows over rough surfaces with complex geometries, including irregular/random roughness [61 69] or scanned real surfaces [10,70 75].
