2. Structure of wall-bounded turbulent flows
2.1. Mean flow over smooth walls
Turbulent flows are irregular in nature, and the variations from one instant to another is substantial; however, the bulk flow can be described by mean flow characteristics. Any expressions and law for the description of the mean flow must, not only express the mean flow pattern but also satisfy the non-slip boundary condition at the wall and the condition of the zero-slope of velocity at the edge of the boundary layer. The bulk properties of mean flow in both smoothand rough-wall boundary layers can be derived by classical asymptotic matching process [21,84,85]. Let d be the thickness of boundary
