low parameters. The M-O similarity is limited to the surface layer and above the roughness sublayer (see Fig. 2 (a)) and probably is not valid for large roughness [129,131]. Recently, Yang et al. [154] reported that the M-O similarity provides good predictions of the mean flow characteristics; however, its evaluation of the turbulent fluctuations is far from satisfactory. Tong and Nguyen [155] showed that the incomplete similarity is related to two assumptions used in M-O similarity: (i) the length scale of the energy-containing eddies only scales with y, and (2) L is only a vertical length scale. Multi-point Monin Obukhov similarity (MMO similarity) was proposed to address the incomplete similarity in the M-O hypothesis [155 158]. More information on MMO similarity can be found elsewhere [155 159]. M-O similarity has received significant attention for predicting the ABL, especially in most state-of-the-art LES models. It issued as a surface boundary condition to calculate the surface Reynolds stress and the turbulent surface sensible and latent heat fluxes, for example, see Ref. [160 162]. A wide range of studies has employed M-O similarity for considering the effect of the surface roughness in LES (for example, see Ref. [163 167]). Several researchers have pointed out that M-O similarity can only be considered valid within the internal layer (see Fig. 2 (a)) and the upper part of the surface layer; however, it fails in the roughness sublayer (see Fig. 2 (a)), where the direct effect of the roughness elements are presented [168 172]. For an unstable condition, the height of internal sublayer y* (top of the roughness sublayer) is corresponded to y* = 35y0, where y0 is the hydrodynamic roughness length [168]. However, Physick and Garratt [170] suggested y* = 50y0 and noted that value of y* might be smaller for stable condition. Garratt [168] and Harman and Finnigan [171] suggested universal functions for below y* so as to accommodate the roughness sublayer. Based on the previous studies, Basu and Lacser [172] suggested employing y* = 50y0 as a general rule for LES models, which also used in wall-modelled LES by Maronga et al. [166]. Recently Couvreux et al. [165] analysed the results of ten LES codes and reported a substantial sensitivity of surface fluxes to the specified hydrodynamic roughness length.
2.3. Townsend hypothesis
Despite these shortcomings, the M-O similarity implies that flow can be divided into two separate regions that seem to not interact with each other. This observation, as well as observations indicated by Townsend [222] and Bradshaw [223], led to the development of Townsend hypothesis that has been widely employed to describe turbulent flow over smooth and rough surfaces. Based on Townsend [173] and Bradshaw [174] observations in the laboratory boundary layer, Bradshaw [175] suggested that large-scale motions near smooth walls have no noticeable influence on the relation between shear stress and velocity profile near the wall. Townsend [173] proposed a general hypothesis that the near-wall turbulent flow can be divided into two turbulent regions: “active” and “inactive” regions. The former region is involved in momentum transfer while the latter is not, and these two turbulent regions do not interact. Active turbulence is generated by shear force and scales on the local parameters of the flow and is responsible for producing the Reynolds shear stress. On the other hand, inactive turbulence is produced by the high energy process far from the surface and scales on outer layer parameters. Townsend hypothesis implies that the inertial turbulent scales (also termed the energy-containing motions) dominate the active region and beyond are large relative to the viscous length scale. The inertial scales are independent of Reynolds number since the upper part of the active region is a fixed fraction of the boundary layer thickness, typically 0.15 d. Townsend [176] described a conceptual model through which the kinematics of boundary layer flow can be statistically explained by the hierarchy of geometrically self-similar attached momentum-carrying motions (or eddies) that are randomly distributed in the flow field and internally dominated. Townsend expressed his Attached Eddy Hypothesis (AEH) as:
“The velocity fields of the main eddies, regarded as persistent, organised flow patterns, extend to the wall and, in a sense, they are attached to the wall.” (Townsend [176], p. 153)
AEH explains that the dominant energy-containing motions in the turbulent boundary layer are due to large eddies attached to the wall. These attached eddies have a population density inversely proportional to their height ranging from a height of O(y) to a height of O(d). He proposed using the self-similar function, s1, to model the velocity field V = (u, v, w) for an eddy centred at (xa,ya,za) and superimposed on a uniform background flow as
