2.4. Wall similarity hypothesis
In order to develop engineering models for the turbulent boundary layer over rough surfaces, knowledge of the structure of these type of flows is required. Current engineering models consider the influence of roughness as small perturbations to the smooth wall boundary layer [80]. Therefore, most of the rough-wall turbulent models have been developed based on the extension of smooth-wall turbulent flow data to the rough-walls. The “wall similarity hypothesis” is an extension to Townsend’s Reynolds number similarity hypothesis [176]. Townsend thoroughly described the general structure of turbulent flows in his book [176], by which he explained his hypothesis and also postulated the Reynolds number similarity expressed as:
“while geometrically similar flows are expected to be dynamically similar if their Reynolds numbers are the same, their structures are also very nearly similar for all Reynolds numbers which are large enough to allow (fully) turbulent flow.” (Townsend [176], p. 53)
Based on Townsend’s Reynolds number similarity, the wall similarity hypothesis for turbulent flow over both rough and smooth surfaces as
“outside the roughness (or viscous) sublayer, the turbulent motion in a boundary layer at high Reynolds number are independent of the wall roughness and the viscosity, except for the role of the wall in setting velocity scale U*, the height Y = y d and the boundary layer thickness d.”
Here, d is the zero-plane displacement. The wall similarity hypothesis is also referred to in the literature as the Townsend Reynolds similarity for rough-walls. Wall similarity was supported with the scaling law for velocity spectra in several overlapping spectral ranges by Perry and Abell [220] and also by Perry and Chong [193] and Perry et al. [221,222]. This hypothesis was later labelled as “wall similarity” by Raupach et al. [21]. The wall similarity hypothesis is also referred to as the outer-layer similarity in the literature. According to the wall similarity, for sufficiently high Reynolds numbers outside the viscous sublayer, the turbulent motions are
independent of surface conditions and viscosity (except for wall shear stress and the boundary layer thickness). This means that the turbulent stress, normalised by the wall shear stress, is universal outside the roughness sublayer. The outer region has been generally thought to be unaffected by roughness, as reported in reviews by Raupach et al. [21] and Jimenez [81]. This implies that the mean flow for both smooth and rough walls obeys the universal velocity defect law in the overlap and outer region of the boundary layer, which is expressed as [21]:
