4. Characterisation of turbulent flow over rough surfaces
As explained in the introduction section, the surface roughness alters the characteristics of turbulent flows. The most significant effect of the wall roughness is increased drag and augmented wallnormal functions near the wall [261,284], which leads to a decrease in the anisotropy of the Reynolds stress [285]. Rough-wall turbulent flows were reviewed by Raupach at al. [21] and Jimenez [81]. They
reported that that the boundary layer over both the smooth and rough walls possess quite different structures controlled by different length scales in the inner layer. However, away from the surface, the low-order statistics are reasonably similar, indicating that the outer layer is unaffected by the surface structure (whether it is smooth or rough). Despite long-term research, no universal method for estimation of friction losses has been developed so far; other than hydrodynamic tests or geometrically resolved DNS [286]. However, some empirical correlations for different types of roughness have been developed based on the experimental and DNS studies which are now reviewed in the following sections. [286].
4.1. Equivalent sand-grain roughness
In the derivation of log-law, it is assumed that the near-wall flow is characterised by the viscosity. However, this is merely true for hydrodynamically smooth surfaces where the average height of the surface roughness elements is less than the thickness of the viscous sublayer. Otherwise, the effect of the roughness on the flow should be considered by defining a roughness scale that reflects the effect of roughness characteristics on the flow. In the literature, the equivalent sand-grain roughness height is widely used for characterisation of roughness in rough-wall flows. The equivalent sand-grain approach stems from the pioneer works by Nikuradse [287,288] and Schlichting [3]. The origin of this approach dates from Nikuradse's sand-grain experiments which provided data on the scaling laws. The concept of this approach is based on the definition of a single length scale which can characterise the roughness. This length scale can be applied in various scaling laws for the velocity field and friction factors. Consider that the surface is covered with a layer of uniform packed spheres as dense as possible, as shown in Fig. 9 (a). The diameter of the sphere is called sand-grain roughness height k for the sake of the similarity of the case with sandpaper. For the type of the surfaces physically similar to the sand-grain in Nikuradse's experiment, the length scale can be described by the sand-grain roughness height. However, when the shape of roughness deviates from the sand-grain, a single parameter cannot describe the sandgrain roughness anymore. In this case, geometrical parameters may be applied to characterise these type of surfaces [82]. Therefore, an equivalent length scale was introduced by Colebrook and White [289] referred to as a so-called equivalent sand-grain roughness ks, as shown in Fig. 9 (b). The equivalent sand-grain roughness is an equivalent roughness height for irregular, and non-uniform rough surfaces that produce the same results as the uniform sand-grain of the Nikuradse for a fully rough flow regime. The definition of the fully rough regime is presented in upcoming sections.
