5.2. CFD studies
CFD has been used by several researchers for the evaluation of the proposed correlations in response to irregular and random roughness. The assessment of various correlations for irregular roughness is limited within the literature. Yuan and Piomelli [64] employed the Large Eddy Smulation (LES) method to evaluate the influence of different roughness correlation on the skin friction factor over four distinctive realistic roughness surfaces. They reported that correlations based on the roughness density [280,281] and roughness slope [283,322] can similarly predict the equivalent sand-grain roughness with better accuracy than moment-based correlations [82,277,320,321]. This is mainly because the spectral moments of the surface do not directly include the information on the slope [66]. This statement along with the aforementioned argument of Flack and Schultz [82] concerning the need for information on the roughness density, demonstrate that at least one parameter containing data on the effect of the roughness density or slope should be included in the methods for estimation of the flow characteristics over rough surfaces.
The equivalent sand-grain roughness height is a roughness scale in the fully rough regime, and is not suitable for the transitionally rough flow regime. The DNS method provides the ability to simulate realistic random roughness with a wide range of surface roughness parameters. Thakkar et al. [72] performed a DNS study for 17 irregular rough surfaces at the same friction Reynolds number (the Reynolds number obtained by applying the friction velocity). All surfaces are scaled to the same physical roughness height under the transitionally rough regime. They showed that the solidity (frontal area ratio), skewness, streamwise correlation length, and root mean square roughness height have a significant influence on the roughness function. According to the above literature review, the equivalent sandgrain roughness appears to be correlated with distinctive sets of geometrical roughness parameters for different rough surfaces, and no universal correlation exists that can predict the flow over surfaces with irregular random roughness. Forooghi et al. [66] performed a DNS study of low Reynold flow over realistic regular roughness. They studied 38 distinctive roughness geometries considering a range of effective slope (0.2 < ES < 0.9), skewness ( 0.33 < sk < 0.66), kurtosis (1.9 < ku < 2.7), and a new parameter D, accounting for the distribution of the roughness element size. They proposed a roughness correlation as
