6. Mean flow over rough walls
As mentioned earlier, current engineering models of the roughwall turbulent models have been developed based on the extension to the smooth-wall turbulent flow models. This section describes the methods for the extension of the mean velocity and temperature profiles over smooth surfaces to that of rough surfaces. The major impact
M. Kadivar et al. / International Journal of Thermofluids 10 (2021) 100077 15


of the roughness is the perturbation of the turbulent boundary layer in such a way that leads to an increase in the wall shear stress, and ultimately, the augmentation of wall heat and mass transfer. This leads to the change of the velocity and temperature profiles near the wall. As shown in Fig. 10, depending on the rough flow regime, the viscous sublayer could partially (in transitionally rough regimes) or completely (in fully rough regimes) be destroyed by roughness. However, according to wall similarity hypothesis, the effect of the roughness on the flow is limited to the roughness sublayer, and the outer layer remains unaffected. In the roughness sublayer, the flow is highly disturbed due to the large turbulence mixing caused by roughness elements, so that three to five roughness heights above the surface is needed where the flow is outside the influence of the roughness elements [21,237,331]. Based on these observations, researchers have attempted to develop models for mean flow over surface roughness, based on the available models of smooth wall flows. Fig. 12 compares the inner-layer mean velocity profile of smooth walls with different rough walls for zero pressure gradient turbulent boundary layer flow. For the rough surfaces, the similarity in the mean velocity profile is shown for all surfaces, while downward displacement (DU+) of the mean velocity profile below the smooth wall (velocity deficit) is observed. The similarity in the mean velocity profile in the overlap and outer layer indicates that the effect of the surface roughness is limited to the inner layer, which is in accordance with the wall similarity hypothesis.
6.1. Mean velocity profile over rough walls
Clauser [292,298] studied the effect of the surface roughness on the mean velocity distribution and presented a method that has demonstrated to be robust and widely applied. He argued that the velocity profile in the viscous sublayer for rough surfaces follows a logarithmic pattern with a similar slope as for smooth surfaces, as illustrated in Fig. 12. He also found that the sole impact of the roughness is a downward shift of the log-law profile as a function of the roughness Reynolds number, as illustrated in Fig. 12. Clauser [298] and Hama [332] each in an independent study introduced a parameter called roughness function (DU+) to emulate the effect of the velocity shift caused by surface roughness. They observed that the surface roughness shifts the logarithmic profile downwards, commensurate with DU+, indicating a shortage in the momentum compared to smooth walls. The velocity shift is proportional to lnksþ [333] and is independent of wall distance [334]. The shift velocity and roughness
function is also a result of wall similarity for characterizing the roughness effect on the mean flow. These have been experimentally validated with numerous experiments including the works of Karen A. Flack and Michael P. Schultz and their colleagues [82,223,235 237,241 251] and also Kenneth T. Christensen and his colleagues [87,224,225,252 257] as well as recent DNS studies [71 73,258 260]. The equilibrium boundary layer flow over rough walls comprises two overlapping regions; an outer layer scaling with u* and d, and an inner layer scaling with n/u* and all length scales required to thoroughly characterise roughness (e.g. roughness height, kurtosis, skewness, etc.). It is evident that the velocity profile also depends on the distance from the wall y itself. However, the definition of the origin of y for rough walls requires careful attention, since the roughness itself dislocates the entire flow in y-direction normal to the wall. To account for this, the displaced height is defined as Y = y d, where d is the “fluid dynamic height origin” or “zero-plane displacement” depending on both flow and roughness characteristics. Clauser [298] proposed the logarithmic velocity distribution for turbulent flow over rough walls as
