7. Roughness effects on fluid flow and heat transfer
As discussed earlier in the paper, roughness can influence heat and momentum transfer in the boundary layer. This influence can be observed through increasing pressure drop, altering flow laminarturbulent transitions, inducing secondary flow motions, improving flow mixing, and enhancing heat transfer. This section briefly discusses three main roughness effects, comprising roughness induced pressure drop, roughness induced laminar-turbulent transition, and roughness induced secondary flow motions. Generally, rough surfaces can be classified into two categories [361,362]: 2D roughness and 3D roughness. The term 2D roughness refers to surfaces with a linear profile such as spanwise or streamwise ribs and groves with different cross-sections which are favourable for heat transfer applications [363 368]. The term 3D roughness refers to surfaces with areal height maps that consist of regular or irregular roughness elements that are systematically/randomly distributed on a surface. A large number of studies have measured the statistical parameters of a wide range of regular and irregular surface roughness fabricated by additive manufacturing [57 59], roughness
generated on the turbine blades [369], and other types of roughness [250,277,341,370 372].
7.1. Roughness induced pressure drop
The form drag and blockage effect induced by roughness elements lead to pressure drop in pipe and channel flows. Early studies focused mainly on the influence of the surface roughness on the pressure drop in pipes and friction losses in boundary layer flows, especially for turbulent flows. Darcy [2] conducted an experimental study of pipe flows with varying roughness, and later, Fanning [373] suggested a correlation for pressure drop in water pipes, including the effect of the wall roughness. These pioneering studies have initiated the field of rough-wall turbulent flows that led to a great deal of theoretical, experimental, and numerical studies in this field. Nikuradse [287,288] coated the inner wall of pipes with uniformly sized sand grains and investigated the effect of wall roughness on the pressure drop for a range of Reynolds numbers and pipe diameters. Nikuradse found that, although surface roughness has a negligible impact on laminar flows, it considerably influences turbulent flows. He also demonstrated that the rougher the surface, the greater the friction, and therefore a greater pressure loss. On the other hand, for flows with specific combinations of relative roughness and Reynolds number categorised as hydrodynamically smooth regimes, the roughness does not affect the flow friction factor. In hydrodynamically smooth regimes, the average height of the surface roughness elements is much less than the thickness of the viscous sublayer; thus the roughness is not able to influence the turbulent flow over the viscous sublayer. Later, Colebrook [340] conducted experimental studies on the effect of roughness on the flow friction factor in pipes of various diameters, with various linings and roughness ranges. He correlated the results in the well-known Colebrook equation as follows:
