7.2. Roughness induced laminar-turbulent transition
Many research studies have been devoted to how roughness can influence the laminar-turbulent transition point, which is of significant practical importance. In turbomachines, most of the blades quickly become rough as a result of deposition as well as mechanical or chemical damage. Surface roughness can modify the flow characteristics by introducing flow instabilities which can have a significant influence on the laminar-turbulent transition location [403,404]. In an attached boundary layer under very low-level flow disturbance (free stream turbulence intensity less than 0.5%) natural laminar-turbulent transition occurs [405]. This process is initiated by the generation of exponentially growing 2D waves known as TollmienSchlichting (TS) waves (primary instability). This is followed by a 3D instability (secondary instability) leading to significant 3D flows with the formation of streamwise/spanwise vortices. The final stage of such a transition process is the breakdown stage. The large scale vortices break down into smaller structures, generating turbulent spots that eventually merge to form a turbulent boundary layer [76,406]. In the presence of certain sources of sufficiently high flow disturbance, for example, freestream turbulence larger than 1% [407] or surface roughness [408], the laminar-turbulent transition occurs more rapidly, and 2D instability stage of natural transition is bypassed. This type of laminar-turbulent transition is referred to as “bypass transition”. Bypass transition comprises three main regions [407]: the buffeted laminar boundary layer, the region of intermittent turbulent spot formation, and the fully turbulent boundary layer. In the buffeted laminar boundary layer, the shear amplification leads to elongated streaks in the streamwise velocity fluctuations, as shown in Fig. 14. In the intermediate region, the localised perturbations initiate instabilities that eventually evolve into turbulent spots. Once the turbulent spots fully formed, they propagate laterally and grow longitudinally until overtake the main turbulent region. Bypass transition was extensively studied by Paul Durbin and his colleagues [407,409 423]. They investigated mechanisms involved in this type of laminar-turbulent transition and developed related numerical models. In general, surface roughness moves the onset of laminar-turbulent transition upstream within the flow by bypassing the primary instability. Smaller levels of roughness destabilize the mean flow through a linear amplification of the primary instabilities [425] while larger ones bypass primary instabilities by distorting the flow locally [408,425,426]. Extensive experimental and CFD studies have been performed on the flow laminar-turbulent transition over different roughness, including isolated roughness as well as 2D, and 3D roughness elements distributed on the surface. Durbin [413] reported that, although a significant body of studies has revealed the aspects of laminar-turbulent transition mechanisms due to isolated roughness [276,427 429], bypass transition caused by distributed roughness has not been fully understood. It is found that the low-speed flow behind an isolated element of roughness lifts up from the wall creating an unstable wake or horseshoe vortices. Then, a laminar-turbulent transition is followed by the algebraic amplification of the individual disturbance evolving into streak-like motions. In addition to this, an unstable detached shear layer over the isolated roughness element further accelerate the transition process. The mechanism of laminar-turbulent transition over distributed roughness is probably related to the formation of streaks by roughness in a similar manner as free stream turbulence flow does. This means that bypass transition may be triggered due to the flow disturbance from the perturbations caused by surface roughness at the wall instead of free-stream turbulence [430]. Fig. 15 illustrates the
