7.3. Roughness induced secondary flow
Secondary flows are the occurrence of noticeable fluid motions perpendicular to the main flow direction in wall-bounded flows. Secondary motions of turbulent flow in non-circular cross-section ducts were first observed by Nikuradse [450] with different types of secondary motions then introduced and categorised by Prandtl [451] into two types: first kind, and second kind secondary flows. Prandtl’s first kind secondary flow occurs in curved channels/pipes of any cross-section geometry due to centrifugal force which prevails in both turbulent and laminar flows. Prandtl’s second kind secondary flow takes place in turbulent flow in non-circular cross-section ducts and is related to inhomogeneities of the Reynolds stresses [452]. It can also occur in plane or symmetrical wall-bounded flows if a local spanwise heterogeneity in wall conditions exists, for example, due to surface roughness. Despite its weak intensity, secondary motion can significantly influence the flow and alter the mean velocity profile due to the generation of local streamwise vorticity. The spanwise inhomogeneity introduces distinct gradients into the Reynolds stress distribution and alters local flow turbulent properties. These gradients lead to an imbalance of local turbulent production and dissipation rate, which in order to fulfil the balance, inherently translates into the generation of local streamwise vorticity [453,454]. This eventually leads to the formation of large-scale vortex pairs occupying the entire boundary layer thickness, as shown in Fig. 17, and can significantly alter the mean velocity profile. While these large-scale features are strongly evident in the averaged velocity field, the corresponding
instantaneous secondary flow fields do not directly reveal their existence [455]. The application of secondary flow for heat transfer enhancement has been studied by researchers; including improved heat transfer by the generation of the secondary flow of Prandtl’s first kind in curved tubes [456 458] and Prandtl’s second kind by roughness [368,459 461]. The formation of secondary motions over surfaces roughened with flow-aligned roughness strips in ducts was demonstrated in the pioneering work of Hinze [453]. Later, Hinze [454] hypothesised that the formation of secondary flows is a consequence of the production dissipation imbalance of turbulent kinetic energy over spanwise heterogeneity. The effect of the secondary flow induced by spanwise inhomogeneity was reported as the thickening and thinning of boundary layer thickness in the cross-plane of the boundary layer [462 466], as shown in Fig. 17. This is due to the formation of low momentum pathways (LMP) associated with upwelling motion and high momentum pathways (HMP) associated with downwelling motions above the surfaces [463,467,468]. In spite of diverse configurations of spanwise inhomogeneity, two main configurations have been well studied in recent decades [69,469,470]: ridge-type roughness and strip-type roughness. Ridgetype roughness consists of spanwise variations of local elevation of the wall, as shown in Fig. 17 (a), while strip-type roughness comprises of spanwise variations in wall-shear stress, as shown in Fig. 17 (b). For ridge-type roughness, the upwelling and downwelling movements were indicated above the elevated and recessed wall areas, respectively [455,464,471 473]. Therefore, secondary flows occur somewhere near the junction between the raised and recessed sections of the surface, with the upwelling either above the junction [470] or above the ridge [466,474,475], as shown in Fig. 17 (a). On the other hand, Strip-type roughness generates upwelling motion over the smooth wall (the low-shear region) and a downwelling motion over the rough walls (the high-shear region) [467,470,476]. Therefore, the secondary flow is placed above the interface between the roughness and smooth surface, as shown in Fig. 17 (b). Previous studies on ridge-type roughness have investigated flat surfaces with the elevated parts comprising of many possible crosssectional shapes: rectangular [466,472,477,478], triangular [471,478,479], semicircle [478], trapezoidal [474], streamwisealigned pyramids [480], and smoother, sinusoidal-like spanwise elevation variations [470,475,481]. Combinations of both ridge-type and strip-type roughness also have been studied in the literature [69,455,464]. Fig. 18 illustrates a schematic of an open channel with
