7.4. The effect of streamwise roughness inhomogeneity
The inhomogeneity in roughness distribution can occur in spanwise direction (as discussed in the previous section), streamwise direction [494,495], or combination of these surface changes [496 498]. Streamwise roughness heterogeneity prevails in numerous engineering wall-bounded turbulent flows. For example, the roughness condition changes occur at the interface between forest and grassland or the bio-fouled patches on the hull of a ship. Such streamwise heterogeneities can take place in a wide range of configuration, among which rough-to-smooth (RTS) and smooth-to-rough (STR), as shown in Fig. 20, are mostly studied in the literature. The surface change triggers various phenomena that lead to the deviation of the near-wall flow from the equilibrium state. The nearwall flow accelerates due to the RTS surface changes [495,499], while it decelerates due to STR surface changes [494,500]. The near-wall flow influenced by the new surface is known as Internal Boundary Layer (IBL) [501 503] and is characterised by a thickness of di(x). The flow away from the wall remains unaffected by surface change and still carries the history from the upstream surface (Fig. 20). The lower part of the IBL that has reached equilibrium with the new surface is referred to as Internal Equilibrium Layer (IEL) and is characterised by a thickness of de(x). The influence of the new surface condition starts from the beginning of the surface change and gradually propagates towards the interior of the flow with transitioning downstream of the flow. In the literature, flow recovery after streamwise-varying roughness has been studied theoretically [504 507], numerically, and experimentally. Wind tunnel experiments have been conducted for flow over a fabricated RTS and STR surface change with roughness geometries composed of square bars [494,495,500,508], mesh roughness [509 511], transversely grooved surface [512], or grit roughness [511]. Krampa-Morlu and Balachandar [513] performed an experimental study of RTS flow recovery with d-type and intermediatetype rib roughness. They showed that the intermediate-type roughness had a higher roughness effect. Cheng and Castro [514] conducted an experimental study of flow field downstream of a step change, where the roughness height becomes ten-fold of the downstream roughness. In the literature, related computational studies have been performed using roughness modelling such as wall-modelled LES, RANS as well as direct resolving of roughness with DNS. The widely used wall model for LES is the equilibrium logarithmic-law of the wall [163,515], and its extension to non-neutral flow using M-O similarity [516,517]. Saito and Durbin [518] developed a wall-modelled LES to investigate the turbulent flow transition over a smooth-roughsmooth step change. They reported an overshoot and undershoot
immediately after the step change, which has been predicted before by other researchers [504,505,519 521]. A few studies have conducted direct resolving of roughness using DNS of flow with different roughness configurations, including 3D sinusoidal surface [522], rib roughness [523 525], and cube roughness [430,526]. Flow recovery over streamwise-varying roughness involves various flow phenomena, two of which are widely addressed in the literature: (i) the departure from equilibrium, and (ii) the formation of an internal boundary layer (IBL).
7.4.1. Departure from equilibrium
Generally, the majority of the flow within IBL is still non-equilibrium with the local wall condition, and a small portion of di is taken up by de (approximately 5 %) [527,528]. Most of the experimental and numerical studies have evaluated the flow characteristics from a certain y+, assumed as departure from equilibrium. Among all, Carper and Porte-Agel [509] conducted an experimental study of RTS surface change at Ret ’ 8800, considering the first measurement point at y+ = 88. Antonia and Luxton [495] and Hanson and Ganapathisubramani [511] studied RTS surface change at Ret ’ 1700 with performing the first measurement at y+ = 95. Jacobi and McKeon [508] conducted the measurements down to y+ = 3 for an RTS surface change at Ret ’ 970 1200. They assumed that the viscous sublayer departed from equilibrium. For high Reynolds number flows, the IEL can be extended to the larger y+. For example, Saito and Pullin [518] performed an wall-modelled LES study of RTS surface change with Ret ’ 2 £ 104 2 £ 106, considering the first grid at y+ = 410. SilvaLopes et al. [515] conducted wall-modelled LES at Ret ’ 1.5 £ 105 with the first grid point at y+ = 260. Rouhi et al. [522] conducted DNS studies of RTS and STR surface changes and showed that log-law assumption for the smooth patch is valid after a higher y+compared to that of rough patch. They found an error of approximately 5 % compared to experiments for velocity profile if the log-layer assumed positioned at y+ = 30. Ismail et al. [526] performed a DNS study of RTS surface change at Ret = 920 with cube roughness and observed the characteristics of smooth wall below y+ = 50.
7.4.2. Internal boundary layer
One of the most important representatives of flow recovery after a surface change is the thickness of the IBL. Various definitions of the IBL have been proposed in the literature (Table 1 of Savelyev and Taylor [503]). In the majority of studies, the growth of di with the streamwise location is described by di = /xb with various values of b ranging from 0.2 to 0.8 proposed based on the adopted definition of IBL. Those studies that employed similar definitions have obtained close values of b. For example, Cheng and Castro [514] and Lee [525] employed the definition of Pendergrass and Arya [529] for an STR surface change at Ret = 2500 and obtained close values of b (0.33 and 0.22). Antonia & Luxton [494] and Win et al. [530] used the definition of Antonia & Luxton [494] for a STR surface change at Ret = 2200 and 2600, and achieved values of 0.72 and 0.8 for b respectively. Li et al. [531] observed the recovery of the viscous region almost immediately to an equilibrium state with the new wall condition. Rouhi et al. [522] observed that the buffer region and beyond takes 5 d (over the smooth patch) and 2.5d (over the rough patch) before recovering to equilibrium conditions. Ismail et al. [523] observed a slow recovery of mean flow and Reynolds stress profile. They divided the flow after an RTS surface change into two zones: (i) initial strong non-equilibrium zone (0 < x/d < 0.5) with sharp change in skin friction proceeding a weak non-equilibrium zone (x/d > 0.5). Ismail et al. [526] suggested a length of an order 50d over the smooth surface after a RTS surface change to recover the Reynold stress. Van Buren et al. [532] studied flow recovery of turbulent flows after RTS surface change in pipes. They reported the recovery of pressure gradient, skin friction and mean streamwise momentum after approximately 20 radii downstream of the surface change, while the turbulent
