8. Conclusion
Turbulent flow over rough surfaces has been studied for about a century through a great deal of experimental and CFD studies. Roughness can influence fluid flow and heat transfer by increasing pressure drop, altering flow regime laminar-turbulent transition, and by inducing secondary flow motions which lead to an enhancement in flow mixing and heat transfer. In most cases, roughness can be quantified by a single scaling parameter, known as equivalent sand-grain roughness height ks, which can be expressed based on the statistical parameters of surface roughness (Ra, sk, ku, ES, etc.). Empirical correlations for the calculation of ks were reviewed in this paper and it was found that methods using only a single parameter or height (Ra or Rq, etc.) were unsuccessful in modelling flows over rough surfaces. Therefore, a series of studies have included the effect of roughness density in the formulation of ks, and alternatively incorporating surface moments such as sk and ku owing to the difficulties in measuring the roughness density in irregular roughness. However, no universal correlation has been proposed so far. The selection of an appropriate correlation for ks is difficult due to the variety of the proposed correlations, which can vary up to a factor of 5 [5]. Other reasons are that ks is a roughness scale in the fully rough regime which is not necessarily useful in the transitionally rough regime, and the wide diversity of roughness types. In addition, the dense roughness regime, which is associated with d-type roughness, does not scale with ks. Therefore, as stated by Flack [286], it is time to move beyond characterising roughness by ks. Another roughness scale is required that can describe the onset of roughness effect and transition to the fully rough regime an well as the shape of roughness function in all roughness regimes. Fluid flow models, particularly those that account for surface roughness, have been developed based on the structure of turbulent flows. Numerous experiments have been conducted, and several theories have been hypothesised to describe flow structure over smooth and rough surfaces. The Monin Obukhov (M-O) similarity hypothesis was one of the initial theories that were proposed based on the mixing length theory to describe vertical distributions of mean flow velocities and temperature using “universal functions”. Experimental studies suggest that M-O similarity is about 10-20 % accurate and can merely be applied to the active region of turbulent flow [129]. On the other hand, Townsend hypothesis thoroughly explains large-scale
motions near smooth in both inner and outer layers and provides a conceptual model of boundary layer kinetics and eddy motions. The wall similarity hypothesis is an extension of the Townsend’s Reynolds number similarity to rough-wall flows, which has been verified by numerous experimental and DNS studies for various roughness regimes and flow conditions, including separated flows. Wall similarity explains that the effect of the roughness is confined in the roughness sublayer and the outer region remains unaffected by roughness. However, evidence of roughness effects into the outer layer has also been observed in several experiments where the ratio between the thickness of boundary layer and roughness height were smaller than 40 [81,235]. Conversely, some other researchers found similarity at lower ratios. Despite progress in finding criteria for the transition from k-type to d-type roughness, a fully accurate distinction between roughness types has not been proposed so far. The proposed criteria include state of the vortex shedding, roughness solidity λ, and effective slope ES. The k-type roughness injects a vortex into the flow above the crest of the roughness elements (vortex shedding) which diffuses into the flow further away from the roughness crests. However, weak vortex shedding has also been observed in d-type roughness. Other studies have associated the k-type with sparse roughness regimes (λ < 0.15) and the d-type with dense roughness regimes (λ > 0.15). A number of recent studies have observed that at effective slopes ES 0.35, that is a delineation between waviness regime (ES < 0.35) and roughness regime (ES > 0.35), is associated with a solidity value of λ = 0.175, approximately close to the value λ = 0.15. The available data from various studies concerning turbulent flow over d-type and intermediate-type roughness does not point to an agreed conclusion or trend, which is mainly related to sheltering effect introduced by these types of roughness. The distribution of mean velocity over rough surfaces can be characterised as a downward shift in mean velocity from the smooth case using a roughness function (DU+) which is solely a function of roughness scale ksþ. However, correlating DU+ with the roughness scale is only valid for sparse roughness regime (k-type roughness). The dense roughness regime (d-type roughness) does not correlate with the roughness scale, whereas it can be scaled with the effective slope (ES). Recent studies suggested employing both roughness scale and effective slope to be able to universally scale turbulent flow in all roughness regimes. In general, roughness can cause an early transition from laminar to turbulent through bypass transition. Although a significant body of study has cast light on the mechanisms of bypass laminar-turbulent transition due to isolated roughness, bypass transition caused by distributed roughness has not been fully understood. However, no apparent effect on the friction factor and the laminar-turbulent transition properties (critical Reynolds number) for relative roughness lower than 1% has been reported [379]. Numerous studies on turbulent flow over rough surfaces have been devoted to heterogeneous roughness which contributes to important engineering applications including heat transfer, ABL flows, aerodynamics, hydraulics, etc. Most studies have investigated streamwise inhomogeneities which cause a transition in the boundary layer, and spanwise inhomogeneities which contribute to the generation of secondary flow motions, while a few studies investigated a combination of both. Although recent experimental and DNS studies have shed light on the different aspects of flow recovery over spanwise surface changes, this area of research is not fully understood. Spanwise heterogeneity resulting from surface roughness have demonstrated the formation of the secondary flow of Prandtls second kind in the turbulent boundary layer. Many of the recent DNS studies have been devoted to investigating this type of secondary flow induced by roughness. The formation of secondary flow is related to the Reynolds stresses' inhomogeneities and leads to the thickening and thinning of boundary layer thickness in the cross
