plane of the boundary layer. There is a wide research gap for investigating scalar transport over both spanwise and streamwise heterogeneous surfaces. The main conclusion of this review paper is that despite many advances being made in respect to the fundamental understanding of turbulent flows over rough surfaces, this subject is far from being comprehensively understood. In almost every study or theory, supporting and conflicting evidence can be found. The main reason is related to the chaotic and random nature of turbulent flows that make accurate predictions almost unattainable. Other reasons are a lack of sufficient systematic studies on the structure of turbulent flow and because of the diverse variety of roughness which significantly impacts the dynamics of the flow in roughness sublayer. Recent advances in additive manufacturing technology, have increased the possibility of fabricating artificial engineered roughness ranging in size from macro to nanoscale [54,112]. This technology provides the designer with the ability to control the shape and pattern of the roughness elements and can be used for the fabrication of roughness to perform systematic experiments. The significant computational power of modern computers and high-performance computing have provided the ability to perform DNS of turbulent flows over realistic roughness. This active area of research seems set for further investigation. Advances in these technologies coupled with a complete synthesised and thorough in-depth review of the domain area, as presented by this paper, can help bridge the knowledge gap in our fundamental understanding of turbulent flows over rough surfaces.
